  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nDay today, some showers arrive late tonight: Weak high pressure\noverhead with pockets of mid cloud and locally dense fog. Thus far\nthe fog is variable in coverage to have anything more than SPS\nout, but will monitor to see if a Dense Fog Advisory is needed \nanywhere. A lull in cloud cover this morning, then clouds increase,\nthicken late today ahead of southern stream shortwave that arrives\nlate tonight. Easterly winds result in warmest readings today \ncentral to north-central WI with highs in the lower, maybe mid \n80s. Few showers make it into southern tier of counties very late\ntonight ahead of the wave.\n\nShowers/Storms Saturday and Saturday night: Trend that is emerging\nis most guidance (except especially the NAM) is to keep majority \nof widespread showers on Saturday to the south closer to the \nhigher instability and convergence on nose of LLJ. This shows up \nnicely when looking at HREF 1hr probs of 0.01\" which shows higher\nprobabilities staying over far southern WI, northern IL during \nthe morning. Increasing moisture and lift on the north edge of \nthis could still produce scattered showers, with the greatest \nfocus southern Fox Valley to the lakeshore. If this trend continues\nthough, some locations could stay dry during the daytime on Saturday,\nwhich looked very unlikely a couple days ago. Main front does \nswing through on Saturday night with primary shortwave over Lake \nSuperior. This is likely when most locations will see at least \nscattered showers. Given boundary is interacting with PWATs over \n1.8\" (95+ percentile) and slower MBE vectors are forecast, \nlocally heavy rain may occur Saturday night. \n\nDry later Sunday into Monday: Front clears eastern WI by Sunday \nmorning. A few showers may linger during the morning, but mainly a\ndry day is expected. Looks kind of breezy with peak gusts over 20\nmph at times as winds shift northwest. Despite being behind the\nfront, it could actually be warmer on Sunday with more sunshine\nand mixing. Not as humid as Saturday, but dewpoints will still \nlinger into the mid 60s over east-central most of the day. Monday\nwill be noticeably less humid across the board. \n\nNext chance for rain is Tuesday...Northwest flow brings another \nshortwave trough and cold front through leading to next chance \nfor organized showers and some thunder Tuesday. Greatest instability\nTuesday afternoon remains southwest of our area, so risk for \nsevere storms looks lower which aligns with various ML \nprojections that shows greater signal southern MN into IA. \nModels/ensembles are picking up on another wave sliding in from \nthe west interacting with the frontal boundary to keep showers and\nsome storms lingering later Tuesday night into Wednesday. With \nPWATs up to 1.5 thrown into the mix, probabilities of 0.5 inch/1.0\ninch of QPF have steadily increased over the last few runs. ECMWF \nEFI/SOT now has some signal for heavy rain. WPC is also now \nindicating a Marginal to Slight (level 1/2 of 4) for excessive \nrain during this time. As we saw with the evolution of this weekends\nhigher risks that eventually shifted south, these areas can shift\nwith time, but will be something to watch as we get closer to this\ntime frame. \n\nCooler air push behind this system for the end of the week with \nhighs settling into the lower to middle 70s.",
